WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.174
Here are 9 keys in a sorted array.

00:00:03.252 --> 00:00:05.271
Binary search finds any of

00:00:05.349 --> 00:00:07.290
them in O(log n) time.

00:00:07.368 --> 00:00:10.941
But inserting or deleting a key means shifting

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:13.349
up to n elements to make room

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:14.980
or to close the gap.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.519
A linked list is the opposite.

00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:19.927
Once you are at the right place

00:00:19.927 --> 00:00:22.554
inserting or deleting is O(1)

00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:23.867
change two links.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.983
But finding that place means

00:00:26.056 --> 00:00:28.610
walking the list, which is O(n).

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.715
A binary search tree gets the best of both.

00:00:31.715 --> 00:00:33.510
It keeps its keys in order

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:34.983
like the sorted array

00:00:35.048 --> 00:00:37.098
Read the tree from left to right

00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:38.892
and you get the array back.

00:00:38.892 --> 00:00:41.007
And it links them with pointers

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:41.840
like a list.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:42.939
Searching,

00:00:42.939 --> 00:00:46.009
finding the smallest or the largest key,

00:00:46.084 --> 00:00:49.753
inserting and deleting all take time proportional

00:00:49.828 --> 00:00:51.775
to the height of the tree.

00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:55.070
This lecture builds each operation in turn.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.920
Recall that a binary tree is either empty

00:00:58.998 --> 00:01:01.256
or a node with a left subtree

00:01:01.334 --> 00:01:02.891
and a right subtree.

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:05.850
A binary search tree is a binary tree

00:01:05.928 --> 00:01:08.420
in which every node holds a key

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.223
and the keys obey the BST property.

00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:14.182
Take any node x, say the root, 8.

00:01:14.259 --> 00:01:16.517
Every key in its left subtree

00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:18.308
is smaller than 8.

00:01:18.386 --> 00:01:20.411
And every key in its right

00:01:20.488 --> 00:01:21.890
subtree is larger.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.634
And this holds for every node

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:26.149
not only the root.

00:01:26.149 --> 00:01:29.181
At 3: 1 on the left is smaller

00:01:29.181 --> 00:01:29.899
and 6

00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:33.410
4 and 7 on the right are all larger.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.484
At 6: 4 on the left

00:01:35.484 --> 00:01:37.080
7 on the right.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.586
Everything to the left of a node is smaller

00:01:40.586 --> 00:01:43.096
everything to the right is larger.

00:01:43.168 --> 00:01:45.033
For now we assume that all

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.467
keys are different.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.404
What to do with equal keys

00:01:48.476 --> 00:01:49.910
comes in part 3.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.555
To talk about trees we need

00:01:52.632 --> 00:01:54.544
a way to write them down.

00:01:54.544 --> 00:01:57.680
The notes use a helper, Trees.shape

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.204
which prints a tree on one line.

00:02:00.204 --> 00:02:02.499
A node is written as its key

00:02:02.575 --> 00:02:04.334
then its left and right

00:02:04.411 --> 00:02:06.553
subtrees in brackets: 8

00:02:06.553 --> 00:02:08.618
then the subtree at 3

00:02:08.618 --> 00:02:10.530
then the subtree at 10.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.437
A leaf is written as just its key: 1

00:02:14.437 --> 00:02:16.808
4, 7 and 13.

00:02:16.808 --> 00:02:20.273
And a dash stands for an empty child

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.370
10 has no left child

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:25.562
so its brackets start with a dash.

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:28.936
14 has a left child, 13

00:02:28.936 --> 00:02:30.760
and no right child.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.840
Programs in the notes print

00:02:32.904 --> 00:02:34.250
their trees this way

00:02:34.314 --> 00:02:36.172
so you can check every result

00:02:36.237 --> 00:02:37.390
against a drawing.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:38.657
In Java

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:42.266
each node stores its key and three links

00:02:42.266 --> 00:02:45.617
to its left child, to its right child

00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:47.250
and to its parent.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.152
A missing child or parent is null

00:02:50.152 --> 00:02:51.804
the slides write NIL.

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:53.756
The root is the only node

00:02:53.831 --> 00:02:55.407
whose parent is null.

00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:58.035
The constructor sets only the key

00:02:58.035 --> 00:03:01.262
so a new node starts with no links at all.

00:03:01.262 --> 00:03:04.340
Searching does not need the parent link

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.343
but successor, predecessor and deletion

00:03:07.418 --> 00:03:10.046
are simpler with it, so we keep it.

00:03:10.121 --> 00:03:12.222
A real tree would also store

00:03:12.298 --> 00:03:13.949
a value with each key

00:03:13.949 --> 00:03:16.426
like a phone number with a name.

00:03:16.426 --> 00:03:18.829
The tree is ordered by the key

00:03:18.829 --> 00:03:20.480
so we keep only that.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.885
Here are two binary trees

00:03:22.958 --> 00:03:26.098
with the same nine keys: tree a and tree b.

00:03:26.098 --> 00:03:28.435
Their shapes are printed below.

00:03:28.435 --> 00:03:31.501
Which of the two is a binary search tree?

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:32.816
Check every node.

00:03:32.816 --> 00:03:34.860
Pause the video and decide.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.718
Tree a is a binary search tree

00:03:40.718 --> 00:03:42.114
every node passes.

00:03:42.114 --> 00:03:43.216
Tree b is not.

00:03:43.216 --> 00:03:45.567
4 is the right child of 6

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.771
but 4 is smaller than 6.

00:03:47.771 --> 00:03:50.709
And so is 3, which sits below 4

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:52.840
in the right subtree of 6.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.084
Here a look at each node and its two children

00:03:56.148 --> 00:03:58.205
was enough to catch the mistake.

00:03:58.205 --> 00:04:00.070
It is not enough in general.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.099
Look at tree c: 10

00:04:02.099 --> 00:04:04.418
with children 5 and 15

00:04:04.418 --> 00:04:07.172
and 5 has children 2 and 12.

00:04:07.172 --> 00:04:10.433
Every left child is smaller than its parent

00:04:10.433 --> 00:04:12.824
and every right child is larger

00:04:12.824 --> 00:04:15.723
5 is less than 10, 15 is more.

00:04:15.723 --> 00:04:18.549
2 is less than 5, 12 is more.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.810
The program agrees: childrenInOrder, true.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.660
But 12 is in the left subtree of 10.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.135
It must be smaller than 10

00:04:28.135 --> 00:04:29.671
and it is larger.

00:04:29.671 --> 00:04:33.000
So tree c is not a binary search tree.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.690
The BST property talks about whole subtrees

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.132
not just children.

00:04:38.208 --> 00:04:40.333
A node must be compared with

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:43.672
every one of its ancestors, however far up.

00:04:43.748 --> 00:04:46.329
Checking only parents and children

00:04:46.404 --> 00:04:48.150
is the classic mistake.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.504
A correct check passes down the range of

00:04:52.599 --> 00:04:56.184
keys that are allowed in each subtree.

00:04:56.184 --> 00:04:58.921
isBst(x, low, high) asks

00:04:59.015 --> 00:05:02.601
is every key in the subtree x strictly

00:05:02.695 --> 00:05:04.677
between low and high?

00:05:04.677 --> 00:05:07.130
An empty subtree is fine.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.737
If x.key is at most low

00:05:09.737 --> 00:05:12.881
or at least high, the answer is false.

00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:15.380
Otherwise check both subtrees.

00:05:15.461 --> 00:05:17.799
Going left from x makes

00:05:17.879 --> 00:05:19.814
x.key the new upper bound.

00:05:19.814 --> 00:05:23.200
Going right makes it the new lower bound.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.932
At the root any key is allowed

00:05:26.008 --> 00:05:28.390
so the first call uses

00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:30.388
Long.MIN_VALUE and Long.MAX_VALUE

00:05:30.388 --> 00:05:33.537
standing in for minus and plus infinity.

00:05:33.537 --> 00:05:35.612
Each node is visited once

00:05:35.612 --> 00:05:38.070
so the check takes linear time.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.111
Run it on tree c.

00:05:40.111 --> 00:05:43.721
At the root 10, the range is everything

00:05:43.721 --> 00:05:46.557
minus infinity to plus infinity

00:05:46.557 --> 00:05:48.190
and 10 is inside.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:49.908
Going left to 5

00:05:49.908 --> 00:05:52.401
10 becomes the upper bound

00:05:52.487 --> 00:05:54.807
5 must lie between minus

00:05:54.893 --> 00:05:56.354
infinity and 10.

00:05:56.354 --> 00:05:57.128
It does.

00:05:57.128 --> 00:05:59.276
Going left again to 2

00:05:59.276 --> 00:06:01.854
the upper bound becomes 5.

00:06:01.854 --> 00:06:02.370
Fine.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.837
Now going right from 5 to 12

00:06:05.837 --> 00:06:08.435
5 becomes the lower bound

00:06:08.435 --> 00:06:11.119
and 10 stays the upper bound.

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:14.410
12 must lie between 5 and 10.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:17.960
It does not, so the check returns false.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.145
15 would have been checked against

00:06:21.218 --> 00:06:23.834
the range from 10 to plus infinity.

00:06:23.907 --> 00:06:25.942
The bound 10 reached 12

00:06:26.015 --> 00:06:27.396
from two levels up

00:06:27.469 --> 00:06:30.086
that is how every node gets compared

00:06:30.158 --> 00:06:31.830
with all its ancestors.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.682
There is a second test

00:06:34.781 --> 00:06:37.448
and it is the reason binary

00:06:37.547 --> 00:06:39.918
search trees are useful.

00:06:40.017 --> 00:06:42.388
Recall the in-order walk

00:06:42.487 --> 00:06:44.660
from Lecture 13

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:48.513
visit the left subtree, then the node

00:06:48.513 --> 00:06:50.884
then the right subtree.

00:06:50.884 --> 00:06:55.132
For tree a it lists 1, 3, 4, 6

00:06:55.132 --> 00:06:58.985
7, 8, 10, 13, 14

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:00.170
increasing.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.585
In a binary search tree

00:07:02.585 --> 00:07:06.457
everything visited before a node is smaller

00:07:06.457 --> 00:07:09.468
and everything after it is larger.

00:07:09.554 --> 00:07:12.308
So a binary tree is a BST if and

00:07:12.394 --> 00:07:15.405
only if its in-order walk lists the

00:07:15.491 --> 00:07:17.642
keys in increasing order.

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:20.826
Tree c lists 2, 5, 12, 10

00:07:20.826 --> 00:07:21.600
15.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:25.373
12 comes before 10: not a BST.

00:07:25.373 --> 00:07:28.014
Tree b fails the same way

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.263
with 6 before 3.

00:07:30.263 --> 00:07:33.198
So the tree is a sorted list

00:07:33.295 --> 00:07:35.838
stored so that we can jump

00:07:35.936 --> 00:07:37.990
into it from the top.

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:41.819
Because a binary search tree keeps its keys

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:44.689
in order while allowing fast changes

00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:47.182
many structures are built on it.

00:07:47.258 --> 00:07:50.959
A database index or a file-system directory finds

00:07:51.035 --> 00:07:53.830
a record without scanning everything.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.550
On disk, each node holds many keys

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.874
those are the B-trees of a database course.

00:07:59.949 --> 00:08:02.367
A stream of prices or timestamps

00:08:02.442 --> 00:08:03.878
arrives one by one

00:08:03.953 --> 00:08:05.842
and at any moment you can

00:08:05.917 --> 00:08:08.486
ask for the smallest, the largest

00:08:08.486 --> 00:08:11.130
or the next key after a given one.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:13.615
And the library collections.

00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:16.132
Java's TreeSet and TreeMap are

00:08:16.208 --> 00:08:18.420
balanced binary search trees

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:19.716
red-black trees

00:08:19.716 --> 00:08:22.080
which come in Lecture 16.

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.471
The methods of TreeSet are exactly

00:08:25.551 --> 00:08:28.040
the operations of this lecture.

00:08:28.121 --> 00:08:30.369
Add 11 keys in any order

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:31.895
and print the set

00:08:31.895 --> 00:08:35.027
the keys come out in increasing order

00:08:35.027 --> 00:08:38.399
because TreeSet walks its tree in-order.

00:08:38.399 --> 00:08:40.006
contains is search.

00:08:40.086 --> 00:08:42.495
first and last are the minimum

00:08:42.575 --> 00:08:43.860
and the maximum.

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.955
higher(15) is 17

00:08:46.955 --> 00:08:50.142
the successor, the next larger key.

00:08:50.142 --> 00:08:52.798
lower(15) is 13

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.304
the predecessor.

00:08:54.304 --> 00:08:56.694
20 is the largest key

00:08:56.694 --> 00:08:59.350
so it has no successor: null.

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.475
And remove is deletion.

00:09:01.564 --> 00:09:05.105
By the end of this lecture you will know

00:09:05.194 --> 00:09:07.850
how each of them works inside.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:11.009
Each operation of a binary search

00:09:11.088 --> 00:09:13.120
tree walks along one path

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:16.012
down from the root or up towards it.

00:09:16.012 --> 00:09:18.200
So each takes O(h) time

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.936
where h is the height of the tree

00:09:21.014 --> 00:09:23.671
the number of edges on the longest

00:09:23.749 --> 00:09:26.016
path from the root to a leaf.

00:09:26.016 --> 00:09:27.110
How big is h?

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.700
These two trees hold the same 5 keys.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.617
In the best case the tree is bushy

00:09:33.698 --> 00:09:36.290
a tree of height h holds at most

00:09:36.371 --> 00:09:39.530
2^(h + 1) - 1 nodes

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:43.095
so h is at least the floor of log₂ n.

00:09:43.095 --> 00:09:44.553
Here that is 2.

00:09:44.634 --> 00:09:46.902
In the worst case every node

00:09:46.983 --> 00:09:48.522
has only one child.

00:09:48.522 --> 00:09:51.682
The tree is a chain, h is n - 1

00:09:51.763 --> 00:09:54.193
and a binary search tree is no

00:09:54.274 --> 00:09:56.380
better than a linked list.

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:57.580
On average

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.981
if the keys are inserted in a random order

00:10:00.981 --> 00:10:03.764
the expected height is O(log n).

00:10:03.841 --> 00:10:06.160
Part 3 shows both extremes

00:10:06.237 --> 00:10:07.861
with 1000 keys

00:10:07.938 --> 00:10:10.411
and Lectures 15 and 16

00:10:10.489 --> 00:10:12.730
keep the height small always.

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:14.086
To sum up.

00:10:14.086 --> 00:10:16.414
1: In a binary search tree

00:10:16.414 --> 00:10:17.655
for every node

00:10:17.733 --> 00:10:21.147
the keys in the left subtree are smaller and

00:10:21.224 --> 00:10:24.405
the keys in the right subtree are larger.

00:10:24.405 --> 00:10:25.802
Two: to check it

00:10:25.802 --> 00:10:29.138
compare every node with all its ancestors

00:10:29.138 --> 00:10:31.078
by passing down a range

00:10:31.156 --> 00:10:33.638
or by checking that the in-order

00:10:33.716 --> 00:10:35.190
walk is increasing.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:39.487
Three: every operation walks one path

00:10:39.487 --> 00:10:41.668
so it costs O(h)

00:10:41.772 --> 00:10:44.680
and h lies between about

00:10:44.784 --> 00:10:47.380
log₂ n and n − 1.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:48.501
Try this.

00:10:48.501 --> 00:10:50.213
Is the tree 20

00:10:50.213 --> 00:10:52.782
with children 10 and 30

00:10:52.782 --> 00:10:56.293
where 10 has children 5 and 15

00:10:56.378 --> 00:10:58.433
and 15 has children

00:10:58.519 --> 00:11:00.488
12 and 25

00:11:00.488 --> 00:11:02.372
a binary search tree?

00:11:02.372 --> 00:11:04.170
Look at 25.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:06.922
In part two: searching for a key

00:11:06.922 --> 00:11:09.033
the minimum and the maximum

00:11:09.106 --> 00:11:10.926
and the successor and the

00:11:10.999 --> 00:11:12.600
predecessor of a node.

